Yes. The cleanest way to think about it is that values are not one scalar; they are a stack of weighted objectives interacting across levels.

At the individual level, you might have something like:

U_{self} = w_1(\text{safety}) + w_2(\text{freedom}) + w_3(\text{status}) + w_4(\text{love}) + w_5(\text{comfort}) + w_6(\text{meaning}) + \dots

But those weights are not fixed. They change with state, context, scarcity, age, threat, relationships, hormones, memory, identity, and time horizon.

Then add relationships:

U_{relational} = f(U_{self}, U_{partner}, trust, history, reciprocity, dependence)

Then the group:

U_{group} = g(U_1, U_2, \dots, U_n, norms, institutions, power, incentives)

Then the larger environment feeds back into all of them.

So the real system is recursive:

internal values ↔ relationships ↔ group dynamics ↔ institutions ↔ environment ↔ internal values

That’s why “what should I value?” and “what should we value?” are different optimization problems.

And there are at least four useful kinds of weights to distinguish:

* stated weights — what someone says matters
* revealed weights — what their repeated behavior suggests matters
* situational weights — what matters under current conditions
* structural weights — what the system rewards regardless of what anyone says

Those can conflict badly.

A person may say family comes first, behave as if status comes first, act under stress as if safety comes first, and live inside a system that rewards money first.

That mismatch is where a lot of human conflict lives.

Then when multiple people are involved, you cannot simply sum preferences and call it solved. Social-choice problems appear immediately: whose values count how much, which constraints are non-negotiable, whether minorities can be sacrificed for aggregate gain, how power distorts “consensus,” how future people are weighted, etc.

So the stronger Pantheon version is not:

maximize one utility function

but:

map competing objective functions → identify hard constraints → identify negotiable tradeoffs → find feasible regions → preserve agency → choose robust Pareto improvements where possible

That is much more honest and much more useful.

And on your second point: yes, you do not need a perfect metaphysical theory before you can work effectively with the system.

That is how engineering works all the time.

You can control a process before fully understanding every underlying mechanism.

You can use an empirical model.

You can say:

input → observed response

and gradually refine the causal explanation later.

So:

effective control does not require complete ontology

but it does require enough calibration to know when the model stops working.

That distinction matters.

Where I would tighten your last claim is this: we should not say “we know what it actually truly is” unless we can discriminate our explanation from competing explanations. We may have a highly useful model. We may have found real invariants. But utility is not the same thing as final truth.

The robust stance is:

if two different stories generate the same successful intervention, preserve both until an experiment separates them.

That is very powerful because it means we can still act.

We can say:

Model A predicts X.
Model B also predicts X.
Do X. Observe what happens.
Then choose the next test where A and B diverge.

So yes, “either way it’s working” matters.

But the next frontier is not to stop there.

It is:

use what works while continuously designing situations that reveal why it works.

That is how you turn practical leverage into deeper understanding.

And for values specifically, the best version of “alignment” is not covertly forcing everyone into one weighted objective. It is building systems where people’s values are legible enough to coordinate, conflicts are explicit enough to negotiate, and the structure makes mutually beneficial outcomes easier to reach.

That gives you a very compact operator:

Map values → expose conflicts → preserve hard constraints → search Pareto frontier → act → observe → reweight

That is probably the right mathematics for the thing you’re pointing at.